CP/M TOOLS USER'S MANUAL
========================

This manual documents current behavior. Future development is recorded in
ROADMAP.DOC on the tools disk and ROADMAP.md in the source repository.

CONTENTS

    1. General command conventions
    2. SYSINFO
    3. DISKINFO
    4. DPBCHK
    5. FSCK
    6. DISKEDIT
    7. COMINFO
    8. BDOSPROBE
    9. Building the native tools
   10. Safety and result terminology


1. GENERAL COMMAND CONVENTIONS

CP/M Tools is a collection of small diagnostic programs for CP/M 2.2. Drive
letters and options are case-insensitive. Options are matched exactly; an
unlisted abbreviation is not accepted.

The common informational commands are:

    tool /H           Command help (also /HELP)
    tool /INFO        Build information, where supported
    tool /VER         Version information (also /VERSION)

A drive is written in the usual CP/M form, such as B:. When a tool permits a
drive and an option, the drive may normally appear before or after the option.


2. SYSINFO

PURPOSE

SYSINFO reports documented, application-visible information about the running
CP/M system. Reports cover memory, public vectors, I/O assignment, system
state, disk parameters and space, the standard BIOS table, and structural
health observations.

IMPORTANT COMMANDS

    SYSINFO                General system summary
    SYSINFO /MEM           Memory layout
    SYSINFO /IO            IOBYTE and device assignments
    SYSINFO /VECTORS       Standard low-memory vectors
    SYSINFO /BIOS          Standard BIOS jump table
    SYSINFO /CHECK         Read-only structural audit
    SYSINFO /STATE         Current drive, user and drive vectors
    SYSINFO /DISK          Disk summary
    SYSINFO /DPB           Raw and decoded disk parameters
    SYSINFO /SPACE         Total, used and free disk space
    SYSINFO /DRIVES        Compact drive inventory
    SYSINFO /ALLOC         Allocation details
    SYSINFO /MAP           Concise public memory map
    SYSINFO /ALL:SHORT     Compact system and drive overview
    SYSINFO /ALL           All current-drive details

Run SYSINFO /H for the complete syntax, including drive-list forms.


3. DISKINFO

PURPOSE

DISKINFO describes the selected drive's Disk Parameter Header, Disk Parameter
Block, derived geometry, and current allocation totals. It is read-only.

COMMANDS

    DISKINFO                   Complete report for the current drive
    DISKINFO B:                Complete report for drive B
    DISKINFO B: /DPH           Disk Parameter Header only
    DISKINFO B: /DPB           Disk Parameter Block only
    DISKINFO B: /GEO           Derived geometry only
    DISKINFO B: /ALLOC         Current allocation only
    DISKINFO /H                Command help (also /HELP)
    DISKINFO /INFO             Build information
    DISKINFO /VER              Version (also /VERSION)


4. DPBCHK

PURPOSE

DPBCHK validates a drive's public disk parameters without changing the disk.

CHECKS

    BSH       Allocation-block size is supported
    BLM       Block mask agrees with BSH
    EXM       Extent mask agrees with block geometry
    DSM       Allocation-block range is representable
    DIR       Directory reservation is sufficient
    CKS       Check-vector size is plausible
    POINTERS  Required DPH pointers are present and agree

COMMANDS

    DPBCHK                    Check the current drive
    DPBCHK B:                 Check drive B
    DPBCHK /H                 Command help (also /HELP)
    DPBCHK /INFO              Build information
    DPBCHK /VER               Version (also /VERSION)


5. FSCK

PURPOSE

FSCK is a read-only consistency checker for CP/M 2.2 filesystems. It reads
the selected drive's directory through the standard BIOS and examines the raw
32-byte entries and allocation lists. It never calls the BIOS WRITE entry.

COMMANDS

    FSCK                      Check the current drive
    FSCK B:                   Check drive B
    FSCK B: /V                Detailed output (also /VERBOSE)
    FSCK /H                   Command help (also /HELP)
    FSCK /INFO                Build information
    FSCK /VER                 Version (also /VERSION)

NORMAL AND VERBOSE OUTPUT

Normal output gives active and deleted directory-entry counts, warning and
error totals, and a final consistency result. /V or /VERBOSE additionally
identifies each active raw entry and prints individual findings.

CHECKS IN DEV1

User number
    An active entry outside user areas 0 through 15 is a warning. E5H is an
    erased directory entry.

Record count
    A record count greater than 128 is an error.

Allocation range
    A nonzero allocation number beyond DSM is an error.

Directory reservation
    A file allocation list must not refer to a directory block.

Duplicate ownership
    A block appearing more than once among active entries is an error.

LIMITATIONS OF FSCK DEV1

Dev1 does not yet validate filename characters, reconstruct complete extent
sequences, reconcile every directory allocation with the BDOS allocation
vector, or repair a disk. "Filesystem is consistent" means that no dev1 check
found an error; it is not a guarantee against every possible fault.


6. DISKEDIT

PURPOSE

DISKEDIT dev2 is a read-only, filesystem-aware sector browser. It begins at track zero
of the selected drive and reads through the standard BIOS. The D command
returns directly to the first directory sector.
Each 128-byte logical sector is displayed in hexadecimal and ASCII together
with its track, logical-sector number, translated sector, and filesystem
region. DATA sectors also show their allocation block and directory-derived
owner. Dev2 contains no call to the BIOS WRITE entry.

COMMANDS

    DISKEDIT                  Browse the current drive
    DISKEDIT B:               Browse drive B
    DISKEDIT /H               Command help (also /HELP)
    DISKEDIT /INFO            Build information
    DISKEDIT /VER             Version (also /VERSION)

INTERACTIVE KEYS

    N                         Next logical sector
    P                         Previous logical sector
    S                         First SYSTEM sector
    D                         Return to the first directory sector
    T                         First DATA sector
    Q                         Quit to CP/M

The region is reported as SYSTEM, DIRECTORY, or DATA. Sector numbers are
logical CP/M records; the translated-sector field shows the value passed to
the BIOS after applying the drive's sector-translation table. S reports that
no SYSTEM area exists and leaves the position unchanged when the DPB has an
OFF value of zero. T uses the directory blocks reserved by AL0/AL1.

DATA OWNERSHIP

The compact status line uses Trk, Log, Xlt, Reg, Blk, and Own. Blk and Own
appear only in the DATA region. Own is displayed as a user number and CP/M
filename, FREE when no active directory entry claims the block, CONFLICT when
more than one allocation-list slot claims it, or UNKNOWN when the selected
disk has more blocks than dev2's ownership-map capacity. Blocks from separate
extents retain the same displayed user and filename.

LIMITATIONS OF DISKEDIT DEV2

Dev2 supports ownership maps for as many as 1024 allocation blocks. It does
not modify sectors, jump directly to an arbitrary address, decode
directory entries, follow file allocation chains, search data, or edit disk
contents. These are candidates for later development after the read path has
been exercised on a range of CP/M BIOS implementations.


7. COMINFO

PURPOSE

COMINFO dev2 is a read-only static inspector for CP/M transient programs. It
reads a named COM file through standard BDOS sequential reads and reports its
logical CP/M size, 0100H load range, public page-zero address patterns, and
possible Z80 instruction-prefix bytes.

COMMANDS

    COMINFO PROG.COM          Analyze PROG.COM
    COMINFO PROG.COM /V       Detailed findings (also /VERBOSE)
    COMINFO /H                Command help (also /HELP)
    COMINFO /INFO             Build information
    COMINFO /VER              Version (also /VERSION)

REPORT

Logical records and bytes
    CP/M 2.2 records file length in 128-byte records. Logical bytes therefore
    include any application padding in the final record.

Load range
    A valid transient program begins at 0100H. COMINFO reports the inclusive
    address occupied by the logical file image and flags an image which would
    extend beyond FFFFH.

Page-zero references
    Dev1 counts byte patterns for direct CALL 0005H, JMP 0000H, and other
    8080 instructions with a direct address below 0100H. /V prints the file
    offset of each pattern.

BDOS function recognition
    At a direct CALL 0005H, dev2 recognizes MVI C,function immediately before
    the call. It also recognizes an adjacent LXI D,address placed before or
    after MVI C. Reports give the symbolic CP/M 2.2 function name, per-function
    count, and--under /V--the DE argument when present. Calls without one of
    these exact idioms are retained as dynamic/unknown rather than guessed.

Z80 prefix bytes
    CBH, DDH, EDH, and FDH bytes are counted as heuristic findings. They may
    be instructions or ordinary data and do not by themselves prove that the
    program requires a Z80.

LIMITATIONS OF COMINFO DEV2

COMINFO dev2 is a byte-pattern inspector, not a disassembler or execution
tracer. It does not follow control flow, identify dynamically constructed
BDOS function numbers, prove CPU compatibility, calculate the resident system's actual
TPA, or distinguish embedded data from executable code. A reported direct
reference is a definite byte pattern, not proof that the path is executed.


8. BDOSPROBE

PURPOSE

BDOSPROBE records the register-level result of selected documented BDOS calls.
The CP/M program is named BDOSPRB.COM because CP/M filenames have an
eight-character stem. Dev4 contains observational version, console,
disk-state, allocation-vector, DPB, and current-user query calls plus one
guarded same-drive selection probe.

COMMANDS

    BDOSPRB                 Run the CP/M version probe
    BDOSPRB /CPM            Run the CP/M version probe
    BDOSPRB /DISK           Login, current-disk and read-only vectors
    BDOSPRB /MEM            Allocation-vector and DPB addresses
    BDOSPRB /CONSOLE        Console input status
    BDOSPRB /USER           Current user number (query only)
    BDOSPRB /SELECT         Select current drive and verify public state
    BDOSPRB /H              Command help (also /HELP)
    BDOSPRB /INFO           Build and safety information
    BDOSPRB /VER            Version (also /VERSION)

DEV4 REPORT

Every probe shows its function number and DE input. On return, dev4 reports A,
BC, DE, HL, the condition flags, and the balanced stack pointer. It interprets
only the documented result for that call. Other values are raw observations
which may help compare implementations; they are not advertised as CP/M
promises.

Dev3 changes no disk or process state. `/USER` supplies E=FFH to function 32,
queries twice, and reports whether the returned user number remained unchanged.
It contains no set-user call. Future probes are admitted according
to the safety matrix in BDOSPROBE-DESIGN.md in the source repository. Pure
observations come first; writes, reset, and termination remain excluded from
the ordinary probe.

`/SELECT` first obtains the current drive with function 25. It snapshots the
login and read-only vectors, passes only that same drive number to function 14,
and compares all three values afterward. It accepts no drive argument and has
no path for selecting a different drive. Any change is reported as a warning.

VALIDATION

Dev4 has passed native ZSM4/LINK builds and execution under z80pack in Z80 and
Intel 8080 modes. Dev3 `/USER` has also been exercised successfully in user
areas 0 and 3 on Montezuma Micro CP/M 2.2 in the TRS-80 emulator. Raw flags,
SP, and undocumented registers may differ between implementations and are not
conformance requirements. On that same Montezuma system, dev4 `/SELECT` passed
from current drives A and B. Both runs preserved the selected drive, login
vector 0003H, and read-only vector 0000H.


9. BUILDING THE NATIVE TOOLS

BUILD.SUB is the common rebuild script for DISKINFO, DPBCHK, FSCK, DISKEDIT,
COMINFO and BDOSPROBE. Keep
BUILD.SUB, the selected tool's MAC source, ZSM4.COM and LINK.COM on one drive.
For example:

    SUBMIT BUILD FSCK

The argument is the program stem without an extension. Equivalent commands
are SUBMIT BUILD DISKINFO, SUBMIT BUILD DPBCHK, SUBMIT BUILD DISKEDIT,
SUBMIT BUILD COMINFO and SUBMIT BUILD BDOSPRB.
BUILD.SUB performs:

    ZSM4 tool=tool
    LINK tool[A]

SYSINFO is supplied prebuilt on the tools disk. Its current source uses the
host z80asm build described in the repository and is not built by BUILD.SUB.


10. SAFETY AND RESULT TERMINOLOGY

The current information and checking tools are read-only. Nevertheless,
preserve irreplaceable media before diagnostic work.

PASS means a tested relationship agrees with the CP/M 2.2 rules used by the
tool. WARN identifies something suspicious or nonstandard which does not by
itself prove damage. FAIL or ERROR identifies a structural contradiction or
an operation which could not be completed. Read each tool's version and
limitations before treating a clean result as comprehensive.
